WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:02.880
Social Security is running out of money.

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:05.980
But don't worry our presidents always say

00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:06.880
they will:

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:09.340
strengthen and save Social Security.

00:00:09.340 --> 00:00:10.500
Safeguard Social Security.

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:11.940
I'll save Social Security.

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:15.740
Except, unfortunately none will really save it.

00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:17.020
Here's the problem.

00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:19.380
Years ago government made us a promise.

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:22.380
Social Security is a program you can count on

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:24.660
now and in the future.

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:27.520
Even if you don't save for your retirement,

00:00:27.529 --> 00:00:29.538
government will save for you.

00:00:29.540 --> 00:00:32.280
We are with you through life's journey.

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:35.880
Social Security: securing today and tomorrow.

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:38.180
But the program doesn't secure tomorrow.

00:00:38.180 --> 00:00:40.120
The politicians misled us.

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:43.400
They didn't save your Social Security payments.

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:45.620
They spent every penny

00:00:45.629 --> 00:00:46.740
the moment they came in.

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:49.080
But most people don't know that.

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:50.240
What happens to that money?

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:52.969
Well hopefully it's held in trust.

00:00:52.969 --> 00:00:55.960
Actually they spent it.

00:00:55.960 --> 00:00:57.580
So what's there gonna be for me?

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:01.420
When politicians began old-age programs,

00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:03.560
they assumed there'd be enough young workers

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:04.480
to tax

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:07.800
to cover the costs of sending checks to old people,

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:09.200
people like me.

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:10.100
But

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:12.080
people like me are living longer these days

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:14.380
so there aren't enough young people

00:01:14.380 --> 00:01:16.000
to pay for us.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:19.040
As a result, Social Security's going broke.

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:20.705
This year it went into the red

00:01:20.705 --> 00:01:21.800
for the first time.

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:23.860
Well there is a plan out there to save it,

00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:26.138
but it requires some tough choices.

00:01:26.138 --> 00:01:28.220
The Heritage Foundation proposes

00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:30.240
cutting payments to rich people

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:33.280
and raising the retirement age to 70.

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:35.017
There are some common-sense tweaks

00:01:35.017 --> 00:01:35.940
that we can make.

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:37.940
Common sense to you, you're young.

00:01:37.940 --> 00:01:39.420
Well you know what happened

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:42.220
is that when Social Security actually was founded,

00:01:42.220 --> 00:01:44.620
life expectancy was below 65.

00:01:44.620 --> 00:01:47.460
The purpose of the program was to protect those few

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:50.280
lucky ones that outlived everybody else.

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:53.100
So today the vast majority live past 65.

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:57.240
Social Security now has a $32 trillion dollar shortfall.

00:01:57.240 --> 00:01:58.940
You expecting to collect Social Security

00:01:58.940 --> 00:01:59.980
from the government?

00:01:59.980 --> 00:02:01.140
No, I doubt it.

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:04.180
I suppose it's progress that this was the first time

00:02:04.180 --> 00:02:06.080
talking to people on the street when

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:07.920
most people understood the problem.

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:09.380
It's just a question of

00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:10.480
how much money

00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:11.420
we're gonna print

00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:12.420
or borrow.

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:14.700
I mean we're already at a trillion dollars a year.

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:19.100
Social Security is kind of like the Titanic,

00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:21.340
a ship heading for an iceberg.

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:24.120
This is a massive ship that moves very slowly.

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:26.320
If we start making reforms now,

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:28.440
we can avoid the iceberg.

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:30.280
If we wait until we can see

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:32.540
the iceberg right in front of our noses.

00:02:32.540 --> 00:02:34.100
and then we try to turn the ship,

00:02:34.100 --> 00:02:36.080
we're gonna run right into it.

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:40.380
Medicare will hit its iceberg in just eight years.

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:43.220
Then benefits will automatically be cut.

00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:46.180
Social Security will hit it in 15 years.

00:02:46.180 --> 00:02:47.660
What's to be done?

00:02:47.660 --> 00:02:49.280
The Democrats say

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:50.860
spend even more.

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:52.500
You don't cut Social Security,

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:54.060
you expand it.

00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:57.880
Nearly every Democrat in the United States Senate

00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:01.880
has voted in favor of expanding Social Security.

00:03:01.880 --> 00:03:05.040
Human wants and needs are unlimited,

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:07.080
there's always another need,

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:09.860
another program that needs more funding.

00:03:09.860 --> 00:03:12.500
And pay for it by taxing rich people.

00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:15.680
That's part of my commitment to raise

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:17.100
taxes on the wealthy.

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:18.320
Just tax the rich.

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:20.280
There isn't enough money even that the

00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:23.000
rich would have to pay for the two hundred trillion

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:25.000
in unfunded liabilities

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:26.960
that we already have on the books.

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:28.460
So were young people worried?

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:30.500
Do you ever think about  Social Security?

00:03:30.500 --> 00:03:32.120
I actually don't. I'm still, I still feel

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:33.860
like I'm too young to know about it.

00:03:33.860 --> 00:03:36.880
When you're old how are you going to support yourself?

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:38.200
I have no idea.

00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:40.680
It's a failed system really because it's almost like a

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:42.100
big Ponzi scheme.

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:45.540
One partial solution proposed by Heritage and others

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:46.460
is to let workers

00:03:46.460 --> 00:03:49.100
put some of their Social Security money into

00:03:49.100 --> 00:03:51.360
personal retirement accounts.

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:55.860
Just imagine being able to own and control

00:03:55.860 --> 00:03:57.560
your own retirement dollars.

00:03:57.560 --> 00:03:59.580
They could invest it in businesses

00:03:59.620 --> 00:04:00.860
that could grow the economy,

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:02.240
whatever rocks your boat.

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:05.220
Private accounts would almost certainly pay you more

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:06.740
than Social Security.

00:04:06.740 --> 00:04:08.780
Even a conservative portfolio

00:04:08.780 --> 00:04:11.980
of stocks and bonds that got you about

00:04:11.980 --> 00:04:14.020
a 5% annual return,

00:04:14.020 --> 00:04:16.540
you would make many times more.

00:04:16.540 --> 00:04:18.040
Private accounts have been tried

00:04:18.040 --> 00:04:19.260
in a few countries.

00:04:19.260 --> 00:04:22.540
The investment they created helped Chile rise

00:04:22.540 --> 00:04:25.220
from one of the poorest countries in Latin America

00:04:25.220 --> 00:04:26.140
to the richest.

00:04:26.140 --> 00:04:29.620
And yet, private accounts are still a hard sell.

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:33.160
Even in Chile, leftists object to

00:04:33.160 --> 00:04:35.680
capitalists getting a cut of the pie.

00:04:36.280 --> 00:04:38.440
The demonstrators are calling for an

00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:39.980
end to the management of pensions

00:04:39.980 --> 00:04:42.200
by private financial institutions.

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:46.260
We lack gratitude

00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:48.100
for what the free market provides.

00:04:48.260 --> 00:04:50.680
That is difficult to wrap your head around.

00:04:50.680 --> 00:04:53.880
It's easy to think, oh here is the government

00:04:53.960 --> 00:04:55.240
this is where I go.

00:04:55.880 --> 00:04:58.180
Private accounts are also unpopular in America.

00:04:58.180 --> 00:04:59.520
We will never

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:01.780
let the Republicans

00:05:01.780 --> 00:05:05.420
cut or privatize Social Security.

00:05:05.420 --> 00:05:09.180
We won't allow cuts to Social Security or Medicare!

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:10.760
What you guys are pushing

00:05:10.760 --> 00:05:13.020
is the right thing to do,

00:05:13.020 --> 00:05:14.760
but it's not popular.

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:16.640
It is not popular but I think

00:05:16.640 --> 00:05:18.140
people don't fully understand

00:05:18.140 --> 00:05:19.460
how these programs work.

00:05:19.460 --> 00:05:21.620
What happens to that money?

00:05:21.620 --> 00:05:23.800
It goes into a pool I guess?

00:05:23.800 --> 00:05:27.140
Do you know they actually don't keep it in the trust fund,

00:05:27.140 --> 00:05:29.900
they spend it right away?

00:05:29.900 --> 00:05:30.900
Didn't know that.

00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:32.280
When I get my paycheck,

00:05:32.300 --> 00:05:34.540
a lot of it goes to taxes and I see the

00:05:34.540 --> 00:05:36.460
Social Security part of it

00:05:36.460 --> 00:05:38.680
and it's like thirty dollars of my whole paycheck.

00:05:38.680 --> 00:05:40.260
You would rather keep that 30 bucks

00:05:40.280 --> 00:05:41.860
and put it into your own savings account?

00:05:41.860 --> 00:05:43.180
Yeah I would.

00:05:43.180 --> 00:05:44.740
The country's in a deficit.

00:05:44.760 --> 00:05:46.220
There's a point where you can't pay out

00:05:46.220 --> 00:05:47.760
and go in the negative forever.

00:05:47.800 --> 00:05:48.560
She's right.

00:05:48.560 --> 00:05:51.800
Far better to fix old age programs now

00:05:51.800 --> 00:05:54.260
rather than have them suddenly go bankrupt

00:05:54.260 --> 00:05:55.180
later.

